{"name":"Coca Mocha","hex":"#bd9d95","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coca","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the four cultivated plants which belong to the family Erythroxylaceae, native to western South America.","The dried leaf of one of these plants, the South American shrub (Erythroxylum coca), widely cultivated in Andean countries, which is the source of cocaine and used as aphrodisiac in the past."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pastry typically made and consumed in the Catalan-speaking areas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coca"},{"term":"mocha","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A coffee drink with chocolate syrup added, or a serving thereof.","A flavour that combines coffee and chocolate."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark brown colour, like that of mocha coffee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mocha"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coca","blocks":[{"text":"Coca is any of the four cultivated plants in the family Erythroxylaceae, native to western South America. It is known worldwide for its psychoactive alkaloid, cocaine. The leaves contain cocaine, which acts as a mild stimulant when chewed or consumed as tea; this traditional use involves slower absorption than purified cocaine, and there is no evidence of addiction or withdrawal symptoms from such natural consumption."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Erythroxylum_novogranatense_var._Novogranatense_%28retouched%29.jpg/330px-Erythroxylum_novogranatense_var._Novogranatense_%28retouched%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coca","wikidata":"Q66793593"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Colcoca02.jpg/250px-Colcoca02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Colco